<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Moundville chiefdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1120 CE – 1450 CE (approx.) · capital Moundville</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the largest Mississippian chiefdoms, centred on a planned town of 29 mounds on the Black Warrior River in Alabama.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1120 farming communities along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-warrior-river">Black Warrior River</a> in west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> began building small mounds at Moundville. By around 1200 the site had been laid out to a deliberate plan: a broad plaza ringed by large platform mounds, the whole enclosed by a wooden palisade, making it the capital of a chiefdom controlling the valley.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Mound pairs around the plaza likely belonged to ranked kin groups, with the paramount chief living on Mound B, some 17 metres high. Only around a thousand people lived inside the town; most farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> in the valley and sent food to the centre. Elite graves contain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> axes, engraved black pottery with hand-and-eye and winged-serpent motifs, and stone palettes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rattlesnake-disk">Rattlesnake Disk</a>. After about 1300 most residents left and Moundville became chiefly a burial and ceremonial centre.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The centre lost its regional authority by about 1450, and smaller chiefdoms took its place; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto</a> met their descendants in 1540. The site is now Moundville Archaeological Park.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/moundville-chiefdom.jpg" alt="Moundville chiefdom" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: The Hsinging Tree · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mothra_Moundville.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1120 CE – 1450 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Moundville</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mississippian religion</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Moundville</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1285&amp;focus=moundville-chiefdom" aria-label="Moundville chiefdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2436 979 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Moundville chiefdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2756 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2756 1235h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Moundville. <a href="/atlas?year=1285&amp;focus=moundville-chiefdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

